<Layout title="Emulation"><PageIntro eyebrow="A line of inquiry" title="Below the abstraction." description="Emulation makes the details matter: state, timing, instructions, and the gap between a model and a machine." /><div class="prose page-prose"><h2>Why emulation?</h2><p>An emulator is a model of behavior. That makes it a rich place to ask precise questions: what is observable, which details matter, and how can an implementation be checked against evidence?</p><h2>Questions for the notebook</h2><ul><li>How can instruction behavior be expressed and tested clearly?</li><li>What does timing change when a functional model seems correct?</li><li>How do small test programs expose a hidden assumption?</li></ul><h2>Evidence before conclusions</h2><p>No emulator results or compatibility claims are published here yet. When there is an experiment to share, it should include its scope, sources, test conditions, and limitations.</p><a class="text-link" href="/blog/">Explore the field notes ↗</a></div></Layout>
